an unprecedented meeting happened this  morning between bishops and survivors of  alleged clergy sexual abuse 12 survivors  from around the world are urging the  Catholic Church to have a zero-tolerance  policy for abuse Niki batiste has been  following this story since last summer  she spoke to some of those survivors and  is in Rome where the meeting has taken  place the meeting ended moments ago and  we just spoke with a survivor on his way  out he said Pope Francis did not make an  appearance so now the question is will  the Pope implement the changes the  survivors want the survivor also told us  he's waited years for this moment and  he's still willing to give the Catholic  Church one last chance I'm not surprised  I'm aggravated this is the CEO of the  Roman Catholic Church  Sean Daugherty said the Pope should have  personally listened to survivors at this  morning's meeting we came to his house  to meet with him about his abuse of  priests and he wasn't there he delegated  Daugherty never imagined his very  personal crusade against Catholic clergy  sexual abuse would lead him to the  Vatican he traveled about 4,000 miles  from Pennsylvania to join eleven fellow  survivors in Rome there's only one  reason you're here only one to get them  to stop doing this to kids since we  first met Daugherty last August he's  fought for a statute of limitations  reform across Pennsylvania and  confronted the former priest he says  molested him I'm at peace right now I'm  completely at peace today's meeting is  the pinnacle in his fight for justice do  you feel that you're carrying the weight  of thousands of survivors on your own  they're carrying me so many people did  so much more than me I'm thrilled to be  a part of that it's known this chance  may never come again survivor Peter I  slay who alleges he was sexually abused  by a priest in Wisconsin at age 13 says  their group's message to Pope Francis is  clear it's really simple zero zero zero  if this summit ends and the Pope doesn't  implement full-on zero  tolerance than what it's going to  accelerate investigations by Justice  Department's and law enforcement around  the world  Sean Daugherty says he believes he's  more likely to win the Powerball than to  see the church make the changes he wants  the Vatican's sexual abuse summit  officially begins tomorrow for CBS this  morning  Niki batiste Rome
 

